#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166434 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "95de2c3e-5810-5aa0-a1d0-b98b012572d4" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Linearity Principle to the Solutions of a Homogeneous System of ODEs"}] :question/id #uuid "e31ad4b2-40e2-5e36-95c0-7a961c53f302" :question/math-academy-id "e-21110" :question/problem "Suppose $\\mathbf{x}_1(t)$ and $\\mathbf{x}_2(t)$ are both solutions of the homogeneous system\n$\\mathbf{x}'(t) = A \\ \\mathbf{x}(t)$.\nWhich of the following is **always** a solution of the same system?\n\n1. $\\mathbf{x}(t) = 6 \\cdot \\mathbf{x}_1(t) - \\mathbf{x}_2(t)$\n2. $\\mathbf{x}(t) = -3 \\cdot \\mathbf{x}_1(t) + 4 \\cdot \\mathbf{x}_2(t)$\n3. $\\mathbf{x}(t) = (1+t^2)\\cdot \\mathbf{x}_1(t) + e^{t}\\cdot \\mathbf{x}_2(t)$" :question/worked-solution "The *linearity principle* for systems of linear ordinary differential equations (ODEs) states that if $\\mathbf{x}_{1}(t)$ and $\\mathbf{x}_{2}(t)$ are solutions to a homogeneous linear system, then for any constants $c_1$ and $c_2$, the linear combination\n$c_{1}\\mathbf{x}_{1}(t)+c_{2}\\mathbf{x}_{2}(t)$\nis also a solution of the system.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given expressions.\n\n- Option I is a solution to the system. Indeed, notice that\n$6 \\cdot \\mathbf{x}_1(t) + (-1) \\cdot \\mathbf{x}_2(t)$\nis a linear combination of the solutions $\\mathbf{x}_{1}(t)$ and $\\mathbf{x}_{2}(t)$ with constant multiplies $c_1=6$ and $c_2=-1$, respectively.\n- Option III is a solution to the system. Indeed, notice that\n$-3 \\cdot \\mathbf{x}_1(t) + 4 \\cdot \\mathbf{x}_2(t)$\nis a linear combination of the solutions $\\mathbf{x}_{1}(t)$ and $\\mathbf{x}_{2}(t)$ with constant multiplies $c_1=-3$ and $c_2=4$, respectively.\n- Option III might be *not* a solution to the system since it's not a linear combination of the solutions $\\mathbf{x}_{1}(t)$ and $\\mathbf{x}_{2}(t)$. Notice that the multipliers of the first and the second terms are not constant.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166435 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3f6920b1-2a94-5a47-840f-c823c9f70e36" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing General Solutions of First-Order Linear Systems of ODEs"}] :question/id #uuid "f74662d2-1414-5716-9eee-2aba2ab7b0fa" :question/math-academy-id "e-21111" :question/problem "Let $\\mathbf{x}_1(t)$ and $\\mathbf{x}_2(t)$ satisfy the homogeneous system $\\mathbf{x}'(t) = A \\ \\mathbf{x}(t)$ and $\\mathbf{x}_p(t)$ satisfy the inhomogeneous system\n$\\mathbf{x}'(t) = A \\ \\mathbf{x}(t) + \\mathbf{f}(t)$.\nWhich of the following is **always** a solution of the same inhomogeneous system?\n\n1. $\\mathbf{x}_1(t) + 2\\cdot \\mathbf{x}_2(t) + \\mathbf{x}_p(t)$\n2. $4\\cdot \\mathbf{x}_2(t) + \\mathbf{x}_p(t)$\n3. $-\\mathbf{x}_1(t) - \\mathbf{x}_p(t)$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if $\\mathbf{x}_h(t)$ satisfies a homogeneous linear system\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}'(t) = A \\mathbf{x}(t)\n$$\n\nand $\\mathbf{x}_p(t)$ is a particular solution to an inhomogeneous linear system\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}'(t) = A \\mathbf{x}(t) + \\mathbf{f}(t)\n$$\n\nthen $\\mathbf{x}_h(t) + \\mathbf{x}_p(t)$ is also a solution to the same inhomogeneous system.\n\nAdditonally, the *linearity principle* for systems of linear ordinary differential equations (ODEs) states that if $\\mathbf{x}_{1}(t)$, $\\mathbf{x}_{2}(t),\\ldots, \\mathbf{x}_{k}(t)$ are solutions to a homogeneous linear system\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}'(t) = A \\mathbf{x}(t)\n$$\n\nthen any linear combination\n$c_1 \\mathbf{x}_{1}(t) + c_2\\mathbf{x}_{2}(t) + \\cdots + c_k\\mathbf{x}_{k}(t)$\nwith $c_1, c_2, \\ldots, c_k \\in\\mathbb R$ is also a solution of the same homogeneous system.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given expression.\n\n- Option I is a solution to the inhomogeneous system. Indeed, notice that the linear combination\n$\\mathbf{x}_h(t) = 1\\cdot \\mathbf{x}_1(t) + 2\\cdot \\mathbf{x}_2(t)$\nis a solution to the homogeneous system $\\mathbf{x}'(t) = A \\mathbf{x}(t)$ according to the linearity principle. Thus,\n$\\underbrace{\\mathbf{x}_1(t) + 2\\cdot \\mathbf{x}_2(t)}_{\\mathbf{x}_h(t)} + \\mathbf{x}_p(t)$\nis a solution to our inhomogeneous system $\\mathbf{x}'(t) = A \\mathbf{x}(t) + \\mathbf{f}(t)$.\n- Option II is a solution to the inhomogeneous system. Indeed, notice that the linear combination\n$\\mathbf{x}_h(t) = 4\\cdot \\mathbf{x}_2(t)$\nis a solution to the homogeneous system $\\mathbf{x}'(t) = A \\mathbf{x}(t)$ according to the linearity principle. Thus,\n$\\underbrace{4\\cdot \\mathbf{x}_2(t)}_{\\mathbf{x}_h(t)} + \\mathbf{x}_p(t)$\nis a solution to our inhomogeneous system $\\mathbf{x}'(t) = A \\mathbf{x}(t) + \\mathbf{f}(t)$.\n- Option III might be *not* a solution to the inhomogeneous system. Notice that $-\\mathbf{x}_1(t) - \\mathbf{x}_p(t)$ can't be written as\n$\\mathbf{x}_h(t) + \\mathbf{x}_p(t)$,\nwhere $\\mathbf{x}_h(t)$ is a solution of the homogeneous system, because of the multiplier $-1$ next to the particular solution $\\mathbf{x}_p(t)$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166436 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "318f129c-066d-50bd-934a-90722ba4fc93" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Image of a Vector Under a Matrix Given Solutions to a Matrix ODE"}] :question/id #uuid "ceb2d89c-fd0b-599f-a5ef-91e4be0d487a" :question/math-academy-id "e-21112" :question/problem "If $\\begin{bmatrix}e^{4t} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$ and $\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ e^{5t}\\end{bmatrix}$ are solutions to $\\mathbf{x}'(t)=A\\mathbf{x}(t)$, find $A\\mathbf{w}(t)$ where $\\begin{bmatrix}4e^{4t} \\\\ 6e^{5t}\\end{bmatrix}$" :question/worked-solution "Since $\\mathbf{u}(t)$ and $\\mathbf{v}(t)$ are solutions to\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}'(t)=A\\mathbf{x}(t)\n$$\n\nthe superposition principle guarantees that $c\\mathbf{u}(t) + d\\mathbf{v}(t)$ is also a solution to\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}'(t)=A\\mathbf{x}(t)\n$$\n\nfor all $c,d\\in\\mathbb{R}$.\n\nNotice that\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{w}(t)=4\\mathbf{u}(t)+6\\mathbf{v}(t)\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\mathbf{w}$ is a solution to\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}'(t)=A\\mathbf{x}(t)\n$$\n\nSo, in order to find $A\\mathbf{w}(t)$ we just need to find $\\mathbf{w}'(t)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nAw &= w^{′}(t) \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}(4e^{4t})^{′} \\\\ (6e^{5t})^{′}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}16e^{4t} \\\\ 30e^{5t}\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167077 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "318f129c-066d-50bd-934a-90722ba4fc93" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Image of a Vector Under a Matrix Given Solutions to a Matrix ODE"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f9197b3a-daef-5748-b56e-01e3af4bfd2f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Aw = \\begin{bmatrix}2\\cos t \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163881} {:answer/id #uuid "03094baa-c772-57fc-9da4-e59e3108116a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Aw = \\begin{bmatrix}-\\cos t - \\sin t \\\\ \\cos t + \\sin t\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163882} {:answer/id #uuid "14ce473c-426f-50b4-9473-734d3ccf55b4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Aw = \\begin{bmatrix}\\cos t + \\sin t \\\\ \\cos t - \\sin t\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163883} {:answer/id #uuid "1914ee99-493c-59e5-b5d2-795d9343f142" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Aw = \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 2\\sin t\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163884} {:answer/id #uuid "74b9889f-55b7-539d-8dcc-2811905ad068" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Aw = \\begin{bmatrix}\\cos t - \\sin t \\\\ \\sin t - \\cos t\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163885}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "14ce473c-426f-50b4-9473-734d3ccf55b4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Aw = \\begin{bmatrix}\\cos t + \\sin t \\\\ \\cos t - \\sin t\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163883} :answer-field/id #uuid "64df5254-9dc1-5914-9459-23645b031940" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163880}] :question/id #uuid "5ff5758f-22cd-54b1-923c-320e4412a990" :question/math-academy-id "q-168100" :question/problem "If\n\n$u(t) = \\begin{bmatrix}\\sin t \\\\ \\cos t\\end{bmatrix}$\nand\n$v(t) = \\begin{bmatrix}\\cos t \\\\ -\\sin t\\end{bmatrix}$\nare solutions to $x^{′}(t) = Ax(t)$, find $Aw(t)$ where\n$w(t) = \\begin{bmatrix}\\sin t - \\cos t \\\\ \\cos t + \\sin t\\end{bmatrix}$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167116 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3f6920b1-2a94-5a47-840f-c823c9f70e36" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing General Solutions of First-Order Linear Systems of ODEs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "88940947-6990-58cc-8351-aa7b410504a7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164065} {:answer/id #uuid "b8631756-4d60-54ed-8af3-c245da4b179e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164066} {:answer/id #uuid "d06558f1-91de-5b73-b834-9ce24df1aa8a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164067} {:answer/id #uuid "2609ca6e-ca57-5060-9b3a-686c8a2b572f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164068} {:answer/id #uuid "673eb9c9-74e2-5a4a-b756-d656f4ed00e4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164069}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2609ca6e-ca57-5060-9b3a-686c8a2b572f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164068} :answer-field/id #uuid "cc1d9fe0-6d54-5dc4-ae06-3959252eb8af" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164064}] :question/id #uuid "e2754510-2110-5629-8492-795ca0ada54d" :question/math-academy-id "q-330290" :question/problem "Let $x_{1}(t)$ and $x_{2}(t)$ satisfy the homogeneous system $x^{′}(t) = Ax(t)$ and $x_{p}(t)$ satisfy the inhomogeneous system\n$x^{′}(t) = Ax(t) + f(t)$.\nWhich of the following is **always** a solution of the same inhomogeneous system?\n\n1. $3 \\cdot x_{1}(t) + x_{p}(t)$\n2. $x_{1}(t) - x_{2}(t) + 2 \\cdot x_{p}(t)$\n3. $-4 \\cdot x_{2}(t) + x_{p}(t)$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167118 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "95de2c3e-5810-5aa0-a1d0-b98b012572d4" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Linearity Principle to the Solutions of a Homogeneous System of ODEs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a9e13cd5-6900-5d2e-855e-829ce82e6c11" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164077} {:answer/id #uuid "b05be18f-87d9-577f-9eb4-3c00a290303e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164078} {:answer/id #uuid "c35789a5-de0c-5348-babd-59e45834d9b1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164079} {:answer/id #uuid "08ddf56e-46eb-5f62-b85b-0d7f5802be9d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164080} {:answer/id #uuid "21bc7045-99fc-5e7f-8081-fbf9814cf8b1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164081}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c35789a5-de0c-5348-babd-59e45834d9b1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164079} :answer-field/id #uuid "9922e169-0eca-553a-8dca-310a49ced5cb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164076}] :question/id #uuid "1e8067e3-26d8-5336-9a9a-e66c696fa115" :question/math-academy-id "q-330348" :question/problem "Suppose $x_{1}(t)$ and $x_{2}(t)$ are both solutions of the homogeneous system\n$x^{′}(t) = Ax(t)$.\nWhich of the following is **always** a solution of the same system?\n\n1. $x(t) = e^{t}x_{1}(t) + x_{2}(t)$\n2. $x(t) = x_{1}(t) + x_{2}(t)$\n3. $x(t) = x_{1}(t) \\cdot x_{2}(t)$ (cross-product)"}]}
